A positive gradient makes the line increase, sloping up from left to right; a negative gradient makes it decrease.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-3.6,0) -- (3.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (0,-3.6) -- (0,3.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-2.6,-2.6) -- (2.6,2.6) node[above left] {positive $m$};
\draw[thick,{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-2.6,2.6) -- (2.6,-2.6) node[below left] {negative $m$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The bigger the gradient size, ignoring its sign, the steeper the line: $y = 5x$ is steeper than $y = 2x$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-3.6,0) -- (3.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (0,-3.6) -- (0,3.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-1.13,-3.4) -- (1.13,3.4) node[above right] {steeper};
\draw[thick,{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (3.4,2.72) -- (-3.4,-2.72) node[below right] {less steep};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Ignore the sign when comparing steepness: $y = -4x$ is steeper than $y = 3x$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-3.6,0) -- (3.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (0,-3.6) -- (0,3.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-1.13,3.4) -- (1.13,-3.4) node[below right] {steeper};
\draw[thick,{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-3.4,-2.72) -- (3.4,2.72) node[above left] {less steep};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
